window.docContents[3].resolve({"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Reflection-functors-as-adjoint-pair/#Chapter7___Exercise7___9___8":{"contents":"Exercise 7.9.8. (a) Let Q be a quiver and let i \\in Q be a source. Let V be a representation of Q and let W be a representation of \\overline{Q}_i (the quiver obtained from Q by reversing arrows at the vertex i). Prove that there is a natural isomorphism between \\operatorname{Hom}(F_i^- V, W) and \\operatorname{Hom}(V, F_i^+ W). In other words, the functor F_i^+ is right adjoint to F_i^-.\n\n(b) Deduce that the functor F_i^+ is left exact and F_i^- is right exact.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.9. Exact functors\tReflection functors as adjoint pair","header":"Reflection functors as adjoint pair","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Reflection-functors-as-adjoint-pair/#Chapter7___Exercise7___9___8"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Exercise2___9___5":{"contents":"Exercise 2.9.5. Show that example (1) is a special case of example (5) (for n = 3).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.9. Lie algebras\tR^3 cross product as special case of so(3)","header":"R^3 cross product as special case of so(3)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Exercise2___9___5"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_-in-characteristic-p/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___4":{"contents":"Problem 2.16.4. Classify irreducible representations of the Lie algebra \\mathfrak{sl}(2) over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic p > 2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.16. Problems on Lie algebras\tIrreducible representations of sl(2) in characteristic p","header":"Irreducible representations of sl(2) in characteristic p","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_-in-characteristic-p/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___4"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Surjective-map-from-direct-sum-of-irreducibles-splits/#Chapter3___Lemma3___1___6":{"contents":"Lemma 3.1.6. There exists a subset J \\subseteq I such that V_J := \\bigoplus_{i \\in J} V_i is mapped isomorphically by f onto U.\n\nProof. Let J be a maximal subset such that f|_{V_J} is injective. If f(V_J) \\neq U, then there exists i \\in I such that f(V_i) is not contained in f(V_J). Then the map V_i \\to U/f(V_J) is nonzero, and hence injective by Schur's lemma. Let J' = J \\cup \\{i\\}; then f is injective on V_{J'}, contradicting the maximality of J, which proves the lemma. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\tSurjective map from direct sum of irreducibles splits","header":"Surjective map from direct sum of irreducibles splits","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Surjective-map-from-direct-sum-of-irreducibles-splits/#Chapter3___Lemma3___1___6"}});
